Wrangler has no setting that mounts assets under a URL prefix. The usual approach is to let your Worker handle `/docs/*`, remove the `/docs` part of the path, and pass the request to the assets binding. Your files stay where they are.

**wrangler.toml**
```toml
[assets]
directory = "./public"        # unchanged
binding = "ASSETS"
run_worker_first = true       # or ["/docs/*"], see note 1
```

**Worker**
```js
export default {
  async fetch(request, env) {
    const url = new URL(request.url);

    if (url.pathname === "/docs") {
      return Response.redirect(`${url.origin}/docs/`, 301);
    }

    if (url.pathname.startsWith("/docs/")) {
      url.pathname = url.pathname.slice("/docs".length); // /docs/a.css -> /a.css
      const res = await env.ASSETS.fetch(new Request(url, request));

      // Asset redirects (e.g. /guide/index.html -> /guide/) point to unprefixed paths; add /docs back
      const loc = res.headers.get("Location");
      if (loc && loc.startsWith("/")) {
        const r = new Response(res.body, res);
        r.headers.set("Location", "/docs" + loc);
        return r;
      }
      return res;
    }

    return new Response("Not found", { status: 404 }); // or your other routes
  },
};
```

Notes:
1. **`true` vs `["/docs/*"]`:** Normally, a request that matches a file is served directly without running your Worker. If you use `run_worker_first = ["/docs/*"]`, your files will also still be reachable at the root (`/style.css` as well as `/docs/style.css`). Use `true` if they should only be available under `/docs`.
2. **Relative links in your HTML** work without changes. **Absolute links** like `href="/style.css"` will skip the prefix. Either build the site with a `/docs` base path (most site generators have a `base` or `basePath` option), or rewrite links in the Worker with `HTMLRewriter`.
3. **Redirect fix:** I wrote the `Location` fix from how asset HTML handling usually behaves (it redirects `/x/index.html` → `/x/`). I haven't confirmed it against the docs, so test it with `wrangler dev`.

The Cloudflare docs confirm two parts of this: how `run_worker_first` works, and that your Worker can pass a request it builds itself to `env.ASSETS.fetch()`. They don't have a page on serving assets under a subpath, so the path-stripping above is the standard workaround, not a built-in feature.

Sources:
- [Static Assets – Configuration and Bindings](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/binding)
- [Static Assets – Routing: Worker script](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/routing/worker-script/)

